## Vocabulary

| Term | Definition | Notes | Reference Location |
|------------------|------------------|-------------------|------------------|
| network(graph) | Set of vertices and edges, G = (V, E) |  | 2 |
| nodes(vertices) | representing entities |  | 2 |
| edges(links/arcs) | representing relationships between entities, Set = Every element of E is in V\*V |  | 2 |
| directed network(digraph) | asymmetric, i.e word A precedes B != B precedes A. Distinguish in-degree and out-dgree |  | 4 |
| in-degree/out-degree | incoming/outgoing edges of each node |  | 4 |
| undirected network | symmetric, If A connects to B, B connects to A (character co-occurence) |  | 4 |
| weight | numerical value, representing strength/frequency/magnitude of relationship |  | 5 |
| unweighted networks | edges are equal whether present (w=1) or absent (w=0). Use when weight unimportant, e.g. coinfection, or friendship |  | 5 |
| weighted networks | gradient information, e.g. \# of eggs in a set of robin nests |  | 5 |
| adjecency matrix | A, a square matrix sized \|V\|\*\|V\|, where A~ij~ == edge weight from nodes i to j (or 1 if unweighted, 0 if no edge). In an Undirected network, A is symmetric (A~ij~ = A~ji~) | one equal way to represent network | 6 |
| edge list | 2 or 3 col table, where each row is one (non-zero occuring) edge. Source, target nodes and weight(optional) | second equal way to represent network, more memory-efficient for sparse | 6 |
| sparse network | many nodes are unconnected | most real world networks | 6 |
| complete graph(network) | every node connected to every other |  | 7 |
| Density | proportion of all possible edges that are present | Complete graph = 1, sparse network is close to 0 | 7 |
| Diameter | length of longest shortest path between any two nodes in the network | small diameter means any two nodes can be reached in few steps | 7 |
| Clustering coefficient (transitivity) | how often 2 nodes that share common neighbor are also connected |  | 7 |
| Connected components | maximal subgraph in which every node can reach every other node. (e.g. all workers at one mill in a set of steel mills) |  | 7 |
